course: biology
lesson: 388
title: Surprising brains: tool-using fish, counting bees, laughing rats and clever pigs
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A honeybee's brain is about the size of a grain of sand, yet in tests bees learned to count landmarks up to about four to find sugar water. Small brains can do surprising things when their cells are well connected.
explain: A tuskfish has no hands, so it carries a clam in its mouth and whacks it on a rock until it cracks: the rock is its anvil. People once thought only humans used tools; now we know birds, apes, otters and even fish do.
explain: Tickled rats make very high chirps, too high for us to hear without special equipment, and come back for more. Scientists think these chirps are a kind of laughter.
explain: Pigs found hidden food using a mirror, and a tiny jumping spider can plan a detour, losing sight of its prey and still sneaking up from behind.
explain: There is no single ladder of cleverness with humans at the top: each animal is clever in the ways its own life needs.
fact: Scientists put a coloured mark on cleaner wrasse fish, and some rubbed it off after seeing it in a mirror; whether this means they know themselves is still argued.
fact: The tuskfish carries a clam in its mouth and smashes it on a rock, using the rock as an anvil.
fact: In tests honeybees learned to fly past a certain number of landmarks, up to about four, to find sugar water.
fact: Bees were reported to learn that an empty card means less than one, a bit like zero, though this is still argued.
fact: Bumblebees were reported to roll little balls again and again with no sugar reward, one of the first reports of play in an insect.
fact: Tickled rats make very high chirps that people cannot hear without special equipment, and they come back for more tickles.
fact: In one study pigs saw food in a mirror and walked round the wall to find the real food, and pigs learned to move a joystick with their snouts in a simple video game.
fact: Some jumping spiders plan a detour, losing sight of their prey before sneaking up from behind; they have eight eyes, and the two big front ones see sharp details.
fact: A honeybee brain has about a million nerve cells, and a human brain about 86 billion.
fact: Crows and parrots pack many nerve cells into small brains, so they are cleverer than their brain size suggests.
fact: Different animals are clever in different ways: a squirrel remembers hundreds of buried nuts, a bee its flower map, a dog our moods.
exercise: What did some cleaner wrasse do after seeing a mark on themselves in a mirror? | Rubbed the mark off | Attacked the mirror again and again | Swam away scared and hid | Tried to eat the mirror
exercise: Why might cleaner wrasse be good at noticing marks? | They spend their lives looking at marks on other fish | They have huge brains, bigger than the brains of sharks | They can see colours no fish can | They live in mirrors
exercise: How does a tuskfish open a clam? | Carries it in its mouth and smashes it on a rock | Pulls it open with its strong front fins, like a starfish does | Waits for it to open by itself in the sun | Asks an octopus to help
exercise: Which of these animals are known to use tools? | Birds, apes, otters and even fish | Only humans | Only humans and the great apes of Africa | No animals at all, only robots
exercise: Up to about how many can honeybees count, in tests? | About four | About a hundred | Only one, no more | About a thousand
exercise: Why would counting be useful to a bee? | Counting landmarks helps it find food and its way home | It counts its own stripes | It needs to count its eggs so it can sell them at the market | It counts people to sting
exercise: What did bumblebees do with little balls in a study? | Rolled them again and again with no reward | Ate them as food | Built nests from them for their eggs | Threw them at each other in a fight over the queen
exercise: What do rats do when tickled? | Make very high chirps, like laughter | Bite the hand of the tickler every time | Curl up and fall fast asleep straight away | Sing loud songs we can hear from the next room
exercise: Why would a scientist need a special microphone to hear a rat laugh? | The chirps are too high for human ears | Rats laugh only under water | Rats laugh very slowly | The chirps are too loud for our ears
exercise: How did pigs use a mirror in one study? | To find food hidden behind a wall | To admire their own faces in the glass | To scare away other pigs from the food | To see much better in the dark barn
exercise: What simple game did pigs learn to play? | A video game moved with a joystick by their snouts | Football with their back legs | Chess with a farmer | Cards held with their ears and their front feet in the barn
exercise: What does a jumping spider do that looks like planning? | Takes a detour, losing sight of prey, then sneaks up from behind | Spins a map out of silk | Calls other spiders for help | Waits forever in one place until a fly lands right in front of its mouth
exercise: How many eyes does a jumping spider have? | Eight | Two | Six | Four
exercise: About how many nerve cells does a honeybee brain have? | About a million | Only about ten or so | About 86 billion | About a hundred or so
exercise: Why are crows cleverer than their brain size suggests? | They pack many nerve cells into small brains | Their brains grow in their wings | They borrow cleverness from people they watch every day | Their brains are made of metal
exercise: Which animal is the cleverest of all, scientists say? | There is no single ladder; each is clever in its own way | Humans, then dogs, then cats, in a ladder | The biggest animal is always the cleverest one of all, so whales win | Insects are cleverest of all
test: In Finding Nemo, Dory the fish forgets everything in seconds. What does science say about some fish? | Some fish use tools, and some seemed to notice themselves in a mirror | Every fish forgets everything in three seconds, just like Dory in the film | Fish have no brains at all | Fish can talk like Dory
test: Count them: 3 jumping spiders. How many eyes in all? | Twenty-four | Six | Eighteen | Thirty-two
test: Count them: a bee has 6 legs and 2 feelers. How many legs and feelers on 4 bees? | 24 legs and 8 feelers | 6 legs and 2 feelers | 24 legs and 24 feelers | 16 legs and 8 feelers
test: «bees are tiny, they can't count nothing» What is right? | In tests they learned to count landmarks up to about four | Bees can count to a million | Bees have no brains | Only queen bees can count, and they count all the eggs in the hive
test: In Ratatouille Remy the rat cooks in Paris. A friend says real rats have no feelings at all. What is right? | Tickled rats chirp like laughing and come back for more | Rats feel nothing ever | Rats laugh so loudly that people have to cover their ears | Rats can cook like Remy if a person trains them
test: What would happen if you tickled a rat and listened with your ears only? | You would probably hear nothing; the chirps are too high | You would hear loud laughter from the next room | It would sing a song | It would bark like a little dog
test: «pigs are dumb, just mud and food» What is right? | Pigs used a mirror to find food and played a joystick game | Pigs cannot learn anything at all | Pigs are blind | Pigs can read books and simple signs on the farm gate
test: «big brain = clever, end of. whale beats everyone» What is the fair view? | How well brain cells are packed and connected matters, not just size | The biggest brain is always the cleverest, so the whale really does win | Size is the only thing that counts | Small brains do nothing
test: «bumblebee rolling a ball, prob just a mistake» What made scientists call it play? | They did it again and again with no reward | They were paid with sugar water each time they rolled one | They were forced to | It happened only once
test: «a fish smashing a clam isn't using a tool, it's just being strong» What is the best answer? | It uses the rock as an anvil to do what it could not alone | Yes, it only uses its strength, so it is not a tool at all | Fish cannot hold anything | The clam breaks itself
test: «my dog reads my moods, my squirrel remembers nuts, which one's smarter, ugh just tell me» What is the best answer? | Each is clever in the way its own life needs | The dog, dogs always win | The squirrel, because its brain is bigger | Neither has any cleverness
exam: Identify the principal manner in which the tuskfish employs an object as an implement. | It strikes a clam against a rock, which serves as an anvil | It grasps a stick with its fins and prises the shell apart | It shapes stones with its teeth | It employs a shell as a hat
exam: Ascertain the approximate number to which honeybees have been shown to count landmarks under experimental conditions. | Four | Forty | One hundred | Only one
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the vocalisations of rats when tickled. | They emit chirps of a pitch above the range of unaided human hearing | They produce a loud bark audible at a great distance across the laboratory | They remain entirely silent | They produce sounds identical to human speech
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the present scientific view of animal intelligence? | Each species is clever in respects suited to its own manner of life | All species may be ranked upon a single ladder with humans uppermost | Intelligence is determined solely by the size of the brain | Insects are devoid of any capacity to learn
